+# Home Blood Pressure: A Three-Morning Guide
+
+## What the numbers mean
+
+Blood pressure describes the force of blood against artery walls. The systolic number is the upper number: it records pressure while the heart's ventricles contract and pump blood out, when arterial pressure is highest. The diastolic number is the lower number: it records pressure between beats while the heart fills, when arterial pressure is lowest. Readings are written systolic over diastolic in `mm Hg`, meaning millimeters of mercury. [NHLBI]
+
+For adults, the packet groups readings as follows. The words in the Relationship column matter: both limits describe Normal and Elevated, while either number can place a reading in Stage 1 or Stage 2. If the two numbers land in different categories, use the higher category when recording the result. [AHA]
+
+| Category | Systolic | Relationship | Diastolic |
+|---|---:|:---:|---:|
+| Normal | Less than 120 | and | Less than 80 |
+| Elevated | 120–129 | and | Less than 80 |
+| Stage 1 hypertension | 130–139 | or | 80–89 |
+| Stage 2 hypertension | 140 or higher | or | 90 or higher |
+
+A single reading is a snapshot and does not confirm a diagnosis. A record over time can give you and your health care professional a more complete picture, but home monitoring does not replace regular appointments. A doctor or another qualified medical professional confirms high blood pressure and helps interpret results in the context of your health. [AHA] [CDC]
+
+## Before each reading
+
+Choose an automatic upper-arm monitor. Place its cuff directly against bare skin rather than over a sleeve; it should be snug, but not excessively tight. Measure at a consistent time so the three mornings are easier to compare, and keep the monitor or written record available for discussion with your health care professional. [AHA] [CDC]
+
+Prepare for the session before sitting down:
+
+- During the 30 minutes before measuring, avoid smoking, alcohol, caffeine, and exercise because they can raise the result. [CDC]
+- Empty your bladder. [CDC]
+- Sit quietly for at least five minutes before starting. [CDC]
+
+These preparation steps reduce avoidable influences on the measurement. If the routine changes on a particular morning, note that context rather than trying to diagnose the meaning yourself. [CDC]
+
+Use the same careful position for both readings:
+
+- Sit in a chair with your back supported. [CDC]
+- Keep both feet flat on the floor and your legs uncrossed. [CDC]
+- Support the cuffed arm on a table at chest height, which places it near heart level. [CDC]
+- Stay still and do not talk while the monitor is measuring. [CDC]
+
+At each morning session, take two readings one minute apart and record both, even if they differ. Body position and recent activity can distort a result; the pair and the short record provide more context than selecting only the reading you prefer. [AHA] [CDC]
+
+## Three-morning record
+
+Use one morning session on each of three days. Take two readings one minute apart: take Reading 1 after the preparation and positioning steps, wait without changing position, and then take Reading 2. Leave the spaces blank until you actually measure; use Notes for relevant context about the session, not for a self-diagnosis. [AHA] [CDC]
+
+| Day | Timing | Reading 1 | Reading 2 | Notes |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Day 1 | Morning | — | — | — |
+| Day 2 | Morning | — | — | — |
+| Day 3 | Morning | — | — | — |
+
+Write both numbers for each result in systolic-over-diastolic form and include `mm Hg`. Keep all six readings rather than deleting an unexpected value. The completed record is information to share with a health care professional, who can advise how often to monitor in the future and what the pattern means for you. [AHA] [NHLBI]
+
+## When to get help
+
+For a reading that is suddenly above 180/120 mm Hg, wait at least one minute and measure again. If the repeated result remains above 180/120 and you do not have the emergency symptoms below, contact a health care professional immediately. This instruction is different from deciding what a lower isolated reading means, and it should not be delayed until the three-morning record is complete. [AHA]
+
+If a reading above 180/120 mm Hg comes with chest pain, shortness of breath, back pain, numbness, weakness, a change in vision, or difficulty speaking, call 911. Do not wait to see whether the pressure comes down on its own. Those combinations are described in the packet as a medical emergency. [AHA]
+
+Home numbers are useful information, not permission to alter treatment independently. Do not stop or change blood pressure medicine because of a home result without consulting a health care professional, regardless of whether the number looks lower or higher than expected. Ask that professional about a personalized target and an ongoing schedule; this handout supplies neither. [AHA]
